# Drop obsolete face rotation when the head becomes pose-derived

Status: DONE
Milestone: Correct rear-facing body with a camera-facing head after face loss
Dependencies: 20_CORE_MOTION_UPGRADE.todo, 26_LIMB_FRAME_TWIST.todo

## Purpose
Use the current pose-derived head when facial tracking is unavailable, without
locking valid independent head turns to the torso.

## Tasks
- [x] Reproduce the exact reported35second frame with continuous tracking/history.
- [x] Distinguish a source detection from a delayed presentation state.
- [x] Add failing-before face-loss, one-frame reacquisition and independent-turn tests.
- [x] Remove obsolete face-specific head/eye local rotation from face-loss interpolation.
- [x] Complete identical-detection recorded-input and native Studio comparison.
- [x] Publish exact evidence and restart guidance; preserve character source and GLBs.

## Acceptance criteria
- [x] With eligible pose head anchors and no face, current local head offsets follow
  the interpolated neck/body rather than a previous face orientation.
- [x] Valid head turns remain independent of shoulders.
- [x] Missing head landmarks do not become tracked or visible merely because of this fix.
- [x] The reported recording frame shows the expected pose-derived head direction.

## Completion evidence
- tests\test_head_tracking_handover.py plus existing rig motion/twist regressions.
- Private comparison work in the Blender3DModels production workspace.
- At34.9706s a brief face estimate reports near-front orientation while the body is rear-facing.
  At35.0038s the face is already unavailable, but delayed local head rotation still faces front.
  Isolated-frame evaluation does not reproduce this; continuous history does.
- docs\evidence\head-handover-validation.json: 374tests/no failures/errors/skips;56focused rig
  regressions; all2209recording frames compared. In502face-loss frames with head geometry,
  obsolete face offsets above one degree fall42to0. Non-head joint matrices remain identical.
- Fresh native rear/front face-loss and independent head-turn cases pass. The first native
  fixture expected torso-only rotation, omitting authored neck tilt; the corrected expectation
  uses the same rest-aware pose derivation without weakening the numeric threshold.
- Model/GLB/video bytes are unchanged. Restart an older running app; no re-export is needed.

## Limits
This does not certify face detections or resolve all monocular front/back pose ambiguity.
No physical camera, user running application, GLB, rig weights or model geometry is changed.

## Completion log
- 2026-10-13 - Reported rear-facing body with a camera-facing head at 35 seconds; reproduced
  with continuous history, corrected the face-loss handover and recorded the comparison.
